A||||17||isthiswhereiputausername?||do you agree with this?  Veteran, 81, charged with making threats at airport||||8||
Z||000000||isthiswhereiputausername?||10-04-2001||12:56 PM||||I live here , I dont agree the guy should have been making a comment like that, but I think the Airport authority went overboard..<P>Veteran, 81, charged with making threats after arrest at city airport<BR> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.theguardian.pe.ca/theprovince.htm#heading2" TARGET=_blank>http://www.theguardian.pe.ca/theprovince.htm#heading2</A> <P>What do you all think of the situation?<P><BR>(Edited: new story there today on it)<p>[This message has been edited by isthiswhereiputausername? (edited 10-05-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||FieroMaster88||10-04-2001||01:01 PM||||Thats a little overboard I think.  What crap!||||reg||13
Z||000002||Formula88||10-04-2001||01:10 PM||||What a load of crap!  How is the question, "Are you looking for a bomb?" a threat?  That's a clear infringement of free speech.  I know people are sensitized right now, but that's ridiculous.  If he'd said, even jokingly, "There's a bomb in her change purse.", that would have been a threat.  He made no threat.  I hope a good lawyer rips them a new one for his pain and suffering.||||reg||13
Z||000003||OcalaFiero||10-04-2001||01:30 PM||||Maybe he should have said "Are you so stupid as to look for a bomb in a friggin' change purse?!?!?" Whjat a load of crap.<P><P>------------------<BR>Mike<BR>Ocala, Florida<BR>'86 2M4<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smallsigpic_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Butter||10-04-2001||01:42 PM||||isthiswhereiputausername,<BR>Sounds like what we have here is a generation gap followed by a lack of respect and common sense.  First off they took a question for a statement. duh! 'Are you looking for a bomb?' and after a quick glance at the old man and woman the answer should have been, Yes, they can come in small enough to be hid as a stamp and sir you could get into big trouble by making that remark hear.  We mean to keep all passengers safe.  If their rules required them to be detained then his record alone should speak for itself.  <P>Our society is going to have to learn how to scrutinize people without discriminating.  Tough row to hoe and we will hear of BS like this till we are able to do so.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DRH||10-04-2001||01:42 PM||||As stated, it sounds like a perfectly legitimate question. If there's not more to it than was reported the airline/airport/security should drop the charges, start apologizing profusely, offer some free tickets and hope they don't get sued.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||10-04-2001||01:45 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>What a load of crap!  How is the question, "Are you looking for a bomb?" a threat?  That's a clear infringement of free speech.  I know people are sensitized right now, but that's ridiculous.  If he'd said, even jokingly, "There's a bomb in her change purse.", that would have been a threat.  He made no threat.  I hope a good lawyer rips them a new one for his pain and suffering.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sounds pretty weak. I don't know if this is a matter of free speech, if meant Constitutionally, but the airport authority overreacted. I don't know the Canadian Constitution or its allowance of speech, but the US Constitution wouldn't cover this forum or arena in regard to free speech. It would cover search and seizure with the 4th, although that's about to change, but free speech in airports has been diminished in airports with the demise of solicitors in airports. Soon the 4th will allow cavity searches in airports. Don't laugh, I'm not kidding. So that's what these terrorists have taken from us; the right to have freedoms and be free from government intrusion. What price do we have/need to pay for safety? At what point is it no longer worth it? If the price is the right to free travel within our borders via checkpoints and often body searches, is absolute safety really worth it? So when I say we need to end terrorism up to and including removing every Arab, as necessary, in the Middle East, does it begin to make sense?||||reg||1
Z||000007||isthiswhereiputausername?||10-04-2001||02:02 PM||||I agree with you Ed on a few points..<P>I hope the governments dont use the terrorists attacks as a reason to take away everyones freedom..in speech or movements..<P><BR>Terrorism should be stopped without "letting them win" by having us all loose our personal freedoms.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||84Bill||10-04-2001||03:01 PM||||A bit testy these days aren't they. <BR>Our government is lying to us. They can't stop stuff like Sep 11th from happening! I mean a guy in Philly international walked in with several razor knives/ box cutters. another man walked through security with a disassembled SHOTGUN AND AMMO!!! <BR>I mean come on people our freedom is being taken and replaced with a false sense of security. Meanwhile back at the ranch everyone is accosted by boneheads with bad attitudes and big egos looking for their first big bust and pouncing on decent law abiding people. <P>Crap like this just burns me up to no end!<BR>||||reg||8
Z||000009||isthiswhereiputausername?||10-04-2001||03:29 PM||||Well..  I still think they should have treated this guy with some respect (Respect your elders dont count any more?)<P>I know I will be one of the first to boycott the airport and aircanada until they drop the charges and make this up to him.. (When we fly, we will spend the extra 2 hrs and drive to Moncton N.B or 5 hrs to Halifax N.S)  All they wanted was  a vacation but instead they got thrown off a flight, flight plans ruined,arrested,treated like a criminal instead of someone who fought for our freedom in WW2.<P>I think they lost all of their common sense  <BR>and agree fully with what you said Bill,  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> Meanwhile back at the ranch everyone is accosted by boneheads with bad attitudes and big egos looking for their first big bust and pouncing on decent law abiding people. <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||8
Z||000010||frontal lobe||10-04-2001||05:09 PM||||Well, she could have been hiding one of those glycerin suppositories in there that elderly often use, and he could have used it to overcome the pilot by poking him in the eye with it.  Those WWII vets are tough, you know.  How idiotic that situation became.  Use a few brain cells, please.  Just a few.<P>I had to fly to Dallas last Friday, and back Saturday.  I was "wanded" both times, and patted down once (by an arab-appearing gentleman.  role reversal going on there!), but happily NO CAVITY SEARCH!!  I'm pretty fine with infringements on my freedom UP TO THAT POINT.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Blondie88||10-04-2001||06:59 PM||||It's sad that they had to make a point with the little old man, and of course the after effects are just upsetting!  I keep thinking of my grandpa tearing up.  AH!!! But if they just let them through after saying that, no matter how stupid, other people would think they could joke about it too...and when do you know when someone's serious?  Someone said something about not being prejudice, don't remember the comment, but isn't it prejudice to arrest 20 year olds when you let the 80 year olds say whatever they want?  Some people think that way...it's weird, but I hear it.  <P>I think everyone's scared shitless, and people just need to not mention the word bomb.  That's the best way to go.  It sucks, but right now, freedom of speech is kinda out the door. Look at politically incorrect!  They're off the air because of what dennis miller said about the U.S being cowards by bombing them.  He lost ALL his sponsors.  It's just something we have to deal with while it's going on, but i'm sure it'll get better soon.  I'm sure in other wars, or problems such as this, the same things happened. <P>I don't mean to say that airport authorities didn't overreact, they did. I can just understand where they're coming from in trying to make a statement, and trying to be careful.  They should have just taken him aside in a little room or something.  They're sure making examples!  I"ll be sure when i'm flying to canada in 2 weeks that I won't mention the word bomb at all!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mach10||10-04-2001||08:22 PM||||Eds: Nope, the airport authority can do pretty much whatever it wants. They can and do detain people for the mis-pronounciation of the word "um."<BR>Otherwise, stuff like that isn't covered under freedom of speach, anyway... But nonetheless, as stated "are you looking for a bomb" is pretty feeble ground... Let's set the scene: Old guy's watching his wife getting her purse ransacked by a couple of haughty sweaty customs dudes. He gets anxious and impatient so he says "What? Are you looking for a BOMB in there?!?"<BR>It's something I'd say if I was getting annoyed at customs. <P>I have only one thing to say about this, which can best be described as:<P>----<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"><BR>----||||reg||8
Z||000013||Cheever3000||10-04-2001||08:43 PM||||What answer was he expecting?  It didn't matter.  They are in uniform so you let them do what they want.  You ain't gettin on the plane without going through the metal detector, and you ain't getting on the plane without having your purse inspected.  Doesn't matter what they're looking for, let em look.<P>And no, you don't say the word bomb in an airport.  Just like you don't tug on Superman's cape, etc.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||EdsB52||10-04-2001||09:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B><BR>----</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"Eds: Nope, the airport authority can do pretty much whatever it wants. They can and do detain people for the mis-pronounciation of the word "um.""<P>They can do what they want here in America as well, but the evidence seized may or may not be admissable against the defendant. There is an issue in regard to the fact that these employees aren't representatives of the government, hence no Constitutional rights are guaranteed. So now that we're going to governmentalize our airport checkpoints, there will be more Constitutional protections.<P>"Otherwise, stuff like that isn't covered under freedom of speach, anyway..."<P>As I stated, not here either.<P>"But nonetheless, as stated "are you looking for a bomb" is pretty feeble ground... Let's set the scene: Old guy's watching his wife getting her purse ransacked by a couple of haughty sweaty customs dudes. He gets anxious and impatient so he says "What? Are you looking for a BOMB in there?!?"<BR>It's something I'd say if I was getting annoyed at customs. <P>I have only one thing to say about this, which can best be described as:<P>----<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"> "<P>Ya I agree. This reaches the proportion of duress words we used in the military. If you were being 'hijacked,' you would state the duress word to subtly alert others that you were in trouble. The problem is that we're going to that level of operations in our civil sector. At what price is freedom worth?||||reg||1
Z||000015||Mach10||10-04-2001||09:25 PM||||Or wedge his undies up his as$-cra--<BR>Ah, nevermind...    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Yes, you don't say the "b" word in an airport. But I DO think that this was a HUGE case of overreaction. 4 weeks ago, they would not have even BLINKED. That's what's sad. Common sense, PEOPLE. The guards KNEW that he wasn't packing, they were just jumpy and eager to put the tough face on someone. Guaranteed the charges will be dropped.<P>Of course, that's ASSUMING that all he had said was "Looking for a bomb in there?"<BR>The article hints that there was more than that. <P>Edit:<BR>lol... Ed's message pops up while I was writing THIS one... I'll just tack on a response:<P>Eds: Best use of "duress words" i've seen was in an old Faulty Towers episode. The cook is dead drunk after Manuel fends off his advances (  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ), and Penny (damn, is that her name?) is trying to relay this to Basil without alarming the rich guests... The dialogue goes like:<BR>"what is it Penny?"<BR>"Oh, cook's just MARINATED the ducks in WHITE WINE"<BR>"Oh? Jolly good!"<BR>"No! No! He's PASSED OUT the potatoes!"<BR>"What the devil are you talking about?"<BR>"He's SOUSED --The vegetables-- UNDER THE TABLE"<BR>"What is WRONG with you?!?"<BR> <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 10-04-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000016||isthiswhereiputausername?||10-04-2001||10:09 PM||||I will keep you all informed of any happenings in this  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"> court case.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Raydar||10-05-2001||10:15 AM||||What a load of crap. <P>I have a colleague named Jack. <BR>Called me on the phone a few months ago. Seeing his name on the caller ID, I just picked up the phone and said "Hi, Jack!!" After a brief pause, I said "Damn. That'll get me in trouble at the airport." <BR>Too true. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||13
